# Generated files, committed because GitHub Pages serves them directly.
#
# Merging a branch that touched a template against one that touched content
# conflicts in the rendered output every time, and resolving it by hand is
# always wrong: the answer is not a blend of the two sides, it is whatever
# `python build.py` produces from the merged sources. The `generated` driver
# resolves to either side without complaint so the merge completes; then you
# re-run the build and commit the real answer.
#
# The driver has to be declared per clone (it is a command, so git will not
# take it from a repository file). Once:
#
#   git config merge.generated.name "generated — rebuild after merging"
#   git config merge.generated.driver true
#
# Without that config git falls back to the normal merge and you get ordinary
# conflicts, which is inconvenient but not dangerous.

/*.html          merge=generated
/sitemap.xml     merge=generated
/robots.txt      merge=generated

# Keyed by content hash, so both sides are internally consistent and neither
# is mergeable. Same rule: take a side, rebuild.
/images/_d/manifest.json merge=generated linguist-generated

# Derivatives are binary; never try to diff or merge them textually.
/images/_d/*.webp binary
